%T A398922 134,174,168,212,182,260,210,284,260,312,280,395,306,396,372,470,380,
%U A398922 536,420,562,518,594,506,738,558,726,656,800,650,912,724,950,828
%N A398922 Number of distinct acute lattice trapezia of area n/2 with horizontal base.
%C A398922 The vertices of these trapezia form the pattern 1221.
%C A398922 a(n) is also the number of skew lattice trapezia of area n/2 whose vertices form the pattern 1212.
%C A398922 a(n) is also the number of solutions to the Diophantine equation a*(b+2c+d) = n, with a,b,c,d >= 1.
%F A398922 Dirichlet g.f.: zeta(s) * (zeta(s-2) - 4*zeta(s-1) + 3*zeta(s) + 2^(-s)*zeta(s))/4.
%e A398922 a(5) = 2: The two trapezia of area 5/2 have vertices {(0,0), (1,1), (2,1), (4,0)} and {(0,0), (2,1), (3,1), (4,0)}.
%e A398922 a(8) = 10: Five with height 1, width 7 and top of length 1, three with height 1, width 6 and top of length 2, one with height 1, width 5 and top of length 3, and one with height 2, width 3 and top of length 1.
%t A398922 a[n_] := Total[Floor[(n/#-2)^2/4]& /@ Select[Divisors@n, #<=n/4&]]; Array[a, 50]
%Y A398922 For partial sums, see A398923.
%K A398922 nonn,new
%O A398922 1,5
%A A398922 _David Bevan_, Aug 14 2026
